I will prove the stronger result that the desired bound holds for all  $n \ge 1,$  i.e.  $x_{i-1}$  and  $x_{i+1}$  may be the same number and they may even both be  $x_i.$  

Let us call a configuration of numbers around a circle *valid* if every number divides the sum of its two adjacent numbers.

First, I prove the lower bound, i.e. that   $$ \frac{k_1+\ldots+k_n}{n} \ge 2. $$   By the AM-GM Inequality, we get
\begin{align*}
    \frac{k_1+\ldots+k_n}{n} &\ge \sqrt[n]{k_1\cdots k_n} 
    &= \sqrt[n]{\left(\frac{x_n+x_2}{x_1}\right)\cdots\left(\frac{x_{n-1}+x_1}{x_n}\right)} 
    &= \sqrt[n]{\frac{(x_1+x_3)\cdots (x_n+x_2)}{x_1\cdots x_n}} 
    &\ge \sqrt[n]{\frac{2\sqrt{x_1x_3} \cdots 2\sqrt{x_nx_2}}{x_1\cdots x_n}} 
    &= \sqrt[n]{\frac{2^n x_1 \cdots x_n}{x_1 \cdots x_n}} 
    &= \sqrt[n]{2^n} 
    &= 2,
\end{align*}
as desired. 

Next, I prove the upper bound, i.e. that   $$ \frac{k_1+\ldots + k_n}{n}<3. $$   I proceed with induction on  $n.$  

For the base case,  $n=1,$  it is easy to see that
\begin{align*}
    k_1 &= \frac{x_1+x_1}{x_1} 
    &= \frac{2x_1}{x_1} 
    &= 2 
    &< 3,
\end{align*}
as desired. 

Now, suppose that the desired result always holds for some  $n=i$  where  $i$  is a positive integer, i.e.   $$ \frac{k_1+\ldots+k_i}{i}<3 $$   for all  $k_1, \ldots, k_i$  of valid configurations of  $i$  numbers  $x_1, \ldots, x_i$  around a circle. Then, let us have some configuration of the numbers  $x_1, \ldots, x_{i+1}$  in some order around a circle. Without loss of generality, let   $$ x_3=\max_j(x_j). $$   If   $$ x_2+x_4 \ge 2x_3, $$   then since  $x_3$  is the maximum, we know that   $$ x_2=x_4. $$   However, proceeding around the circle, we find that   $$ x_1=x_2=\ldots=x_{i+1}, $$   which gives
\begin{align*}
    \frac{k_1+\ldots+k_{i+1}}{n} &= \frac{2 \cdot n}{n} 
    &= 2 
    &< 3,
\end{align*}
as desired, so let us assume from here on that   $$ x_2+x_4=x_3. $$   Now, let us consider what happens if we delete  $x_3,$  making  $x_2$  and  $x_4$  adjacent numbers. Then, observe that
\begin{align*}
    \frac{x_1+x_4}{x_2} &= \frac{x_1+(x_3-x_2)}{x_2} 
    &= \frac{x_1+x_3}{x_2}-1 
    &= k_2-1 
    &\in \mathbb{Z},
\end{align*}
and we similarly find that   $$ \frac{x_2+x_5}{x_4} \in \mathbb{Z}, $$   so we have a valid configuration of  $i$  numbers around a circle. Let  $h_1, \ldots, h_i$  denote the new quotients for this configuration. By the inductive hypothesis, observe that   $$ h_1+\ldots+h_i<3i. $$   Also, we have
\begin{align*}
    h_1+\ldots+h_i &= k_1+\ldots+k_{i+1}-\frac{x_1+x_3}{x_2}-\frac{x_2+x_4}{x_3}-\frac{x_3+x_5}{x_4}+\frac{x_1+x_4}{x_2}+\frac{x_2+x_5}{x_4} 
    &= k_1+\ldots+k_{i+1}+\frac{x_4-x_3}{x_2}-\frac{x_2+x_4}{x_3}+\frac{x_2-x_3}{x_4} 
    &= k_1+\ldots+k_{i+1}-1-1-1 
    &= k_1+\ldots+k_{i+1}-3.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we get
\begin{align*}
    k_1+\ldots+k_{i+1} &= h_1+\ldots+h_i+3 
    &= (3i-1)+3 
    &= 3(i+1)-3,
\end{align*}
concluding the inductive step. 

Hence, by the Principle of Induction, I have proven that   $$ \frac{k_1+\ldots+k_n}{n}<3 $$   for all valid configurations of  $n$  numbers around a circle. 

Combining the lower and upper bounds, I have shown that   $$ 2 \le \frac{k_1+\ldots+k_n}{n}<3 $$   for all valid configurations of  $n$  positive integers around a circle, as desired.  $\square$

