<details><summary>solution</summary>Note that  $u(k)=\frac{k}{2^{v_2(k)}}$ , so
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2^n} \sum_{k = 1}^{2^n} \frac{u(k)}{k} &= \frac{1}{2^n}\sum_{k=1}^{2^n}\frac{1}{2^{v_2(k)}} 
&=\frac{1}{2^n}\left(\frac{2^n}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{2^0}+\frac{2^n}{2^2}\cdot\frac{1}{2^1}+\cdots+\frac{2^n}{2^n}\cdot\frac{1}{2^{n-1}}+\frac{2^n}{2^n}\cdot\frac{1}{2^n}\right)
&=\left(\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2k+1}\right) + \frac{1}{2^{2n}}
&=\frac{1}{2}\left(\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^k\right)+\frac{1}{2^{2n}} 
&= \frac{1}{2}\cdot \frac{(\frac{1}{4})^n-1}{\frac{1}{4}-1} + \frac{1}{2^{2n}} 
&= \frac{2^{2n+1}-2}{2^{2n}\cdot 3}+\frac{1}{2^{2n}}
&=\frac{2^{2n+1}+1}{2^{2n}\cdot 3} 
&>\frac{2^{2n+1}}{2^{2n}\cdot 3} = \frac{2}{3}
\end{align*}
This concludes the problem.  $\square$</details>

