The answer is  $f(x)=\boxed{\frac{x^3-x^2-1}{2x^2-2x}},$  which works. We have
\begin{align*}
f(x)+f\left(1-\frac{1}{x} \right)&=x+1 
f\left(1-\frac{1}{x} \right)+f\left(-\frac{1}{x-1} \right)&=2-\frac{1}{x} 
f\left(-\frac{1}{x-1} \right)+f(x)&=1-\frac{1}{x-1}
\end{align*}
so by dividing the sum of the three equations by  $2$  and subtracting the second equation, we obtain the desired result.  $\blacksquare$
